{"title":"Pages for everyone","slug":"code/site/site-everyone","files":{"lesson.md":"---\norder: 1\nunit: real\ntitle: \"Pages for everyone\"\nsummary: \"Check a page works for everyone: pictures described with alt, headings as the page's outline, labels joined to their boxes, links that say where they go, readable colours; then test it with a classmate.\"\nlanguage: html\nvoice: { name: af_heart, speed: 1.1 }\napp:\n  command: node \"$REPO_ROOT/tools/serve.mjs\" \"$REPO_ROOT/dist/_playground-html\" 8130\n  url: http://127.0.0.1:8130/index.html\n  ready: HTML code\n  layout: full\npacing:\n  keyDelayMs: 45\nexplainPicture: page.html#explain\nexplainClip:\n  query: { code: \"@page.html#explain-start\", zoom: 1.6 }\n  caption: \"A page works for everyone when its tags say what each part is\"\n  clip:\n    - caption: \"This page looks fine. A screen reader finds a picture with no words.\"\n    - wait: 1200\n    - caption: \"alt describes the picture for anyone who can't see it.\"\n    - type: { into: HTML code, text: \" alt=\\\"A gold trophy\\\"\", after: \"<img src=\\\"trophy.svg\\\"\" }\n    - caption: \"The box has no name. A label, joined by for and id, gives it one.\"\n    - type: { into: HTML code, text: \"<label for=\\\"name\\\">Name</label>\\n\", after: \"width=\\\"80\\\">\\n\" }\n    - type: { into: HTML code, text: \" id=\\\"name\\\"\", after: \"<input\" }\n    - expect: { selector: \"#preview-text\", contains: \"Name\" }\n    - caption: \"It looks almost the same, and now everyone can use it.\"\n    - wait: 1400\nhook: \"About one in five people has a disability. A page built with the right tags works for screen readers, keyboards and phones as well as for you.\"\n---\nNot everyone uses a page the way you do. Some people hear it through a **screen reader**, which reads the tags aloud; some use only a **keyboard**; some see it small or with poor contrast. A page works for all of them when its tags say what each part is. The checklist:\n\n1. **Pictures** have `alt` text that says what they show (or `alt=\"\"` if they are only decoration).\n2. **Headings** are the page's outline: one `<h1>`, then `<h2>`, then `<h3>`, never chosen for their size.\n3. **Boxes** have a `<label>` joined by `for` and `id`.\n4. **Links** say where they go: not \"click here\".\n5. **Colours** are easy to read: dark text on a light background, or the other way round.\n6. **The keyboard** reaches everything: press Tab through the page.\n\nThe same list works for a hand-built page, a Bootstrap page or a Vue app: they all end up as tags in the browser.\n\n## Predict\n\n```step\nstage: predict\ncode: page.html#predict\ninsight: \"Students assume a screen reader can see the picture; hearing that it reads the file name makes alt text matter.\"\ncheck:\n  type: predict\n  q: \"A screen reader reaches the picture, then the link. What does it say?\"\n  options:\n    - \"Something like \\\"trophy dot svg, image\\\", then \\\"Click here, link\\\"\"\n    - \"\\\"A gold trophy\\\", then \\\"Library meeting room, link\\\"\"\n    - \"Nothing: it skips pictures and links\"\n    - \"It describes the picture's colours\"\n  actual: \"With no alt text, many screen readers read the file name. And \\\"Click here\\\" says nothing about where the link goes, especially when a screen reader lists all the links on a page.\"\n```\n\nNeither the picture nor the link says what it is.\n\n## Run\n\n```step\nstage: run\ncode: page.html#run\ntry: { playground: html, starter: \"page.html#run\", height: 420 }\ncheck: { type: reflect, text: \"I clicked into the preview and pressed Tab: the link, the box and the button each got a focus outline in turn.\" }\nunlocks: [alt-text, heading-outline]\n```\n\nThis version passes the checklist. Click into the preview, then press **Tab** again and again: the keyboard moves through the link, the box and the button. Click the word **Name**: the cursor jumps into its box.\n\n## Investigate\n\n```step\nstage: investigate\ncode: page.html#before\npicture: page.html#before\ninsight: \"Most fixes are the right tag or attribute, not a new look: alt, heading levels, labels and link text.\"\ntasks:\n  - check:\n      type: match\n      q: \"This version looks almost the same but fails the checklist. Match each problem with its fix.\"\n      pairs:\n        - [\"<img src=\\\"trophy.svg\\\"> has no words\", \"add alt=\\\"A gold trophy\\\"\"]\n        - [\"<h4>When we meet</h4> comes straight after the h1\", \"make it an h2, the next level of the outline\"]\n        - [\"<p>Name</p> above the box\", \"a label with for=\\\"name\\\", and id=\\\"name\\\" on the input\"]\n        - [\"Click here\", \"link text that says where it goes: library meeting room\"]\n      explain: \"Each fix is a tag or an attribute that tells the browser what the part is. The page looks the same; it works for more people.\"\n  - check:\n      type: bug\n      code: page.html#bug\n      text: \"A screen reader user jumps between headings to find the parts of this page, and hears levels 1, 4, 4: it sounds as if two levels are missing. Click the two lines that are wrong.\"\n      answer: [2, 4]\n      fault: design\n      misconception: heading-for-size\n      explain: \"h4 was chosen because it looks small. Headings are the page's outline: the parts under the h1 are h2. Make them smaller with CSS if you want.\"\n```\n\n### Find the fixes\n\nFour problems, four fixes.\n\n### Spot the bug\n\nHeadings chosen for their size.\n\n## Modify\n\n```step\nstage: modify\ninsight: \"Fixing a page for everyone is small, exact changes to tags and attributes.\"\ntry: { playground: html, starter: \"page.html#fix\", height: 420 }\ntasks:\n  - hint:\n      - \"Each picture needs an alt that says what it shows, for someone who can't see it.\"\n      - \"Add alt=\\\"…\\\" inside each img tag, describing that picture.\"\n    expected: page.html#sol-alt\n    check: { type: code, text: \"Give both pictures alt text.\", rules: { contains: [\"star.svg\\\" alt=\\\"\", \"medal.svg\\\" alt=\\\"\"] } }\n  - hint:\n      - \"A label names a box. for on the label and id on the box must match.\"\n      - \"Swap each p for a label with for=\\\"…\\\", and give its input the same id.\"\n    expected: page.html#sol-labels\n    check: { type: code, text: \"Give both boxes a label joined with for and id.\", rules: { contains: [\"<label for=\", \"<input id=\"] } }\n```\n\n### Describe the pictures\n\nalt for each one.\n\n### Name the boxes\n\nLabels joined to their boxes.\n\n## Make: check your own page\n\n```step\nstage: make\ninsight: \"Goals only: apply the checklist to their own page, then test it with a real person.\"\ntry: { playground: html, starter: \"page.html#make\", height: 460 }\ncheck: { type: reflect, text: \"I checked my own page against all six points, a classmate tested it, and I fixed the three biggest problems.\" }\n```\n\nPaste in a page you have made, from any course. Then:\n\n1. Go through the **six points** above and fix what you find.\n2. **Test it with a classmate.** Give them one job (join the club, find the meeting time, answer the first question) and watch without helping. Then ask them to do it again with only the keyboard. Note every place they hesitated.\n3. Fix the **three biggest** problems they found.\n\n## Where this leads\n\n```step\nstage: info\ncheck:\n  type: question\n  q: \"Your page passes the checklist and a classmate has tested it. What is the next step to let anyone use it?\"\n  options: [\"Put it online, at an address you can share\", \"Add more colours\", \"Turn off the keyboard\", \"Print it\"]\n  answer: 0\n  explain: \"Next lesson: download the page and its files, check the paths, and put it online.\"\n```\n\nNext: **put it online**.\n\n```teacher\nsummary: Students predict what a screen reader says for a picture with no alt\n  and a \"click here\" link, move through an accessible page with the keyboard,\n  match problems to fixes, find headings chosen for size, fix pictures and\n  labels, then check their own page and test it with a classmate.\ntime: 55\ntiming: Predict and Run 10 min · Investigate 10 min · Modify 10 min · Make\n  (check and classmate test) 25 min\ngoals:\n  - I can check a page for alt text, headings, labels, link text, contrast and\n    keyboard use.\n  - I can fix a page so a screen reader and a keyboard can use it.\n  - I can test my page with a real person and fix what they find.\nbefore: >\n  The lesson works on any page, from any route through the Web pathway:\n  Bootstrap and Vue pages end up as ordinary tags in the browser, so the same\n  checks apply. Nothing new is taught about HTML: alt, headings, labels and\n  links all appear in HTML & CSS; this lesson is about **why** they matter and\n  **checking** for them.\n\n\n  The checklist follows the most common failures in automated checks of real\n  home pages (missing alt text, missing form labels, low contrast and empty or\n  vague links top the WebAIM Million survey every year). Contrast is hard to\n  judge by eye: in Chrome or Edge, right-click the page, Inspect, and the colour\n  picker shows a contrast ratio (4.5 or more for normal text). Screen readers:\n  Narrator (Windows, Ctrl + Windows + Enter) or VoiceOver (Mac, Command + F5)\n  let students hear their page; turn them off the same way.\n\n\n  The classmate test is the important part: a five-minute test with one person\n  finds more problems than a checklist. Students give one task and watch without\n  helping.\nrunning:\n  - \"Predict: if you can, play a screen reader reading a page with no alt\n    (Narrator or VoiceOver).\"\n  - \"Run: everyone presses Tab through the preview. Ask what the outline around\n    each part is for.\"\n  - \"Investigate: for the bug, ask how a screen reader user moves around a page\n    (by headings).\"\n  - \"Modify: ask students to say each alt text aloud: does it say what the\n    picture shows?\"\n  - \"Make: pair students. The tester only tries; the maker only watches and\n    notes, then fixes.\"\nstruggle: Students think a page that looks right is right. Ask \"how would\n  someone who can't see it, or can't use a mouse, do this?\" and let them try\n  with only Tab and Enter.\nstuck:\n  - see: Clicking the label does not put the cursor in the box.\n    ask: Do for and id match exactly, capitals included?\n    fix: 'Use the same word in both: for=\"name\" and id=\"name\".'\n  - see: Tab skips a button or link.\n    ask: Is it a real button or a tag (a, input) that can take focus, or a div made\n      to look like one?\n    fix: Use button or a; a clickable div is invisible to the keyboard.\n  - see: Long alt text that repeats the caption.\n    ask: What would you say to a friend on the phone about this picture?\n    fix: A short description; alt=\"\" if the picture is only decoration.\nstretch: Run Chrome's Lighthouse accessibility check (Inspect, Lighthouse) on a\n  real website and on your page. Listen to your page with Narrator or VoiceOver.\nsupport: Give students the six points as a printed tick sheet for their own page.\nleads: \"Next lesson: put the page online. For Digital Technologies, this lesson\n  covers designing the user experience and testing it with users (Years 9 and\n  10, AC9TDI10P07). Class idea: everyone tests a classmate's page with only the\n  keyboard.\"\nexit:\n  q: Name two changes that make a page work for a screen reader without changing\n    how it looks.\n  a: \"For example: alt text on pictures, labels joined to boxes, headings in\n    order, link text that says where it goes.\"\nexplain: |\n  1. The page looks fine, but a screen reader finds a picture with no words.\n  2. `alt` describes the picture for anyone who can't see it.\n  3. The box has no name: a `label`, joined by `for` and `id`, gives it one.\n  4. It looks almost the same, and now everyone can use it.\nsources:\n  - title: \"WebAIM: The WebAIM Million (accessibility of the top million home pages)\"\n    url: https://webaim.org/projects/million/\n    checked: 2026-10-12\n  - title: \"W3C WAI: Easy checks, a first review of web accessibility\"\n    url: https://www.w3.org/WAI/test-evaluate/preliminary/\n    checked: 2026-10-12\n  - title: \"ABS: 5.5 million Australians have disability (Survey of Disability,\n      Ageing and Carers, 2022)\"\n    url: https://www.abs.gov.au/media-centre/media-releases/55-million-australians-have-disability\n    checked: 2026-10-12\nsolutions:\n  step-4#t1: page.html#sol-alt\n  step-4#t2: page.html#sol-labels\n```\n","page.html":"<!-- region: predict -->\n<img src=\"trophy.svg\" width=\"80\">\n<p><a href=\"https://example.com/rooms\">Click here</a></p>\n<!-- endregion -->\n\n<!-- region: run -->\n<h1>Chess club</h1>\n<img src=\"trophy.svg\" alt=\"A gold trophy\" width=\"80\">\n<h2>When we meet</h2>\n<p>Wednesdays at lunch, in the <a href=\"https://example.com/rooms\">library meeting room</a>.</p>\n<h2>Join</h2>\n<label for=\"name\">Name</label>\n<input id=\"name\" type=\"text\">\n<button>Join</button>\n<!-- endregion -->\n\n<!-- region: before -->\n<h1>Chess club</h1>\n<img src=\"trophy.svg\" width=\"80\">\n<h4>When we meet</h4>\n<p>Wednesdays at lunch. <a href=\"https://example.com/rooms\">Click here</a> for the room.</p>\n<h4>Join</h4>\n<p>Name</p>\n<input type=\"text\">\n<button>Join</button>\n<!-- endregion -->\n\n<!-- region: bug -->\n<h1>Robotics club</h1>\n<h4>What we build</h4>\n<p>Robots that follow lines.</p>\n<h4>When we meet</h4>\n<p>Fridays after school.</p>\n<!-- endregion -->\n\n<!-- region: fix -->\n<h1>Art show</h1>\n<img src=\"star.svg\" width=\"60\">\n<img src=\"medal.svg\" width=\"60\">\n<h2>Sign up</h2>\n<p>Your name</p>\n<input type=\"text\">\n<p>Your year</p>\n<input type=\"number\">\n<button>Sign up</button>\n<!-- endregion -->\n\n<!-- region: sol-alt -->\n<h1>Art show</h1>\n<img src=\"star.svg\" alt=\"A star\" width=\"60\">\n<img src=\"medal.svg\" alt=\"A medal for the best painting\" width=\"60\">\n<h2>Sign up</h2>\n<p>Your name</p>\n<input type=\"text\">\n<p>Your year</p>\n<input type=\"number\">\n<button>Sign up</button>\n<!-- endregion -->\n\n<!-- region: sol-labels -->\n<h1>Art show</h1>\n<img src=\"star.svg\" width=\"60\">\n<img src=\"medal.svg\" width=\"60\">\n<h2>Sign up</h2>\n<label for=\"name\">Your name</label>\n<input id=\"name\" type=\"text\">\n<label for=\"year\">Your year</label>\n<input id=\"year\" type=\"number\">\n<button>Sign up</button>\n<!-- endregion -->\n\n<!-- region: make -->\n<!-- Paste a page you have made here (from HTML & CSS, Bootstrap, the DOM course or Vue),\n     then check it against the list in the lesson and fix what you find. -->\n<h1>My page</h1>\n<!-- endregion -->\n\n<!-- region: explain-start -->\n<h1>Chess club</h1>\n<img src=\"trophy.svg\" width=\"80\">\n<input type=\"text\">\n<!-- endregion -->\n\n<!-- region: explain -->\n<h1>Chess club</h1>\n<img src=\"trophy.svg\" alt=\"A gold trophy\" width=\"80\">\n<label for=\"name\">Name</label>\n<input id=\"name\" type=\"text\">\n<!-- endregion -->\n"}}